# Pointer reasoning

A pointer object and the object it designates are different objects. That sentence is simple; a surprising amount of confusion disappears once it becomes automatic.

```c
int x = 10;
int *p = &x;
*p = 25;
```

After the last statement, the stored value of `x` is `25`. The expression `*p` gives access to the designated object.

```quiz
{
  "id": "q-pointer-01",
  "type": "single-choice",
  "prompt": "After executing the snippet above, what is the value of x?",
  "options": [
    {"id":"a","label":"10"},
    {"id":"b","label":"25"},
    {"id":"c","label":"The address of p"}
  ],
  "answer": "b",
  "explanation": "p designates x, so assigning through *p changes x."
}
```

